# =============================================================================
# .gitattributes - gemma-translator
#
# This repo is developed on Windows 11 and deployed to Raspberry Pi OS
# (Linux ARM64). The rules below make sure a given file has the SAME line
# endings and diff/merge behavior no matter which platform touched it last,
# and that platform-specific files (shell scripts vs. .bat/.ps1) get the
# line ending their own interpreter actually requires.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Default behavior
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Let Git auto-detect text vs. binary, normalize text files to LF in the
# repository itself, and convert to the platform's native ending on
# checkout - unless a more specific rule below pins it explicitly.
* text=auto

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Forced LF - files that run on / are read by Raspberry Pi OS (Linux)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These MUST stay LF no matter what platform edited them last. A CRLF
# shebang line breaks on Linux with "bash: bad interpreter: No such file or
# directory" because the trailing \r is read as part of the interpreter
# path. This is not optional for anything that runs on the Pi.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
# The theme of Plymouth. A CR in gemma.plymouth becomes part of the value of
# ModuleName, thus plymouth-set-default-theme looks for "script\r.so", says
# that it does not exist, and the panel keeps the console. The overlay of the
# GPIO and the rules of udev fail the same way, in the kernel and in udev.
*.plymouth      text eol=lf
*.script        text eol=lf
*.dts           text eol=lf
*.rules         text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.editorconfig   text eol=lf

# Extensionless scripts: git attribute patterns can't express "no extension"
# reliably, so there is nothing to glob here. Every current entry-point
# script in this repo (start.sh, setup.sh, download_model.sh, deploy-pi.sh)
# already has a .sh extension and is covered by the *.sh rule above. If an
# extensionless script is ever added, list it here explicitly, e.g.:
#   /bin/some-tool  text eol=lf

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Forced CRLF - Windows-only files
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# cmd.exe's batch parser is genuinely line-ending sensitive - multi-line
# constructs (FOR loops, IF/ELSE blocks, labels) can misbehave or fail to
# parse with bare LF. Batch files only ever run under cmd.exe, so there's no
# cross-platform reason to keep them LF; force the ending they need.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# *.ps1 is intentionally NOT forced to CRLF. PowerShell's parser (Windows
# PowerShell 5.1 and cross-platform PowerShell 7/pwsh alike) reads
# LF-terminated scripts correctly. Forcing CRLF here would reintroduce the
# exact platform-coupling problem this file exists to avoid, for no benefit.
# Left to `* text=auto` (stored as LF, checked out native).

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. C# / .NET source and project files
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.csproj    text
*.props     text
*.targets   text
*.config    text
*.axaml     text
*.xaml      text
*.resx      text

# *.sln - forced CRLF.
# Research finding: this is no longer a hard technical requirement. Modern
# `dotnet` CLI tooling and Visual Studio 2019/2022 parse LF-only .sln files
# without complaint - the strict "VS will corrupt/refuse LF solutions" bugs
# were a VS2015-and-earlier era problem. That said, CRLF-for-.sln remains
# the de facto community convention (it's still what `dotnet new sln` and
# VS itself emit on save on Windows), some older VS extensions / installers
# are still less forgiving, and this file is only ever opened or generated
# on a Windows dev box in this project - never on the Pi. Forcing CRLF costs
# nothing here and matches what most tooling will write back anyway, so it
# stays pinned rather than left to `text=auto` drift.
*.sln       text eol=crlf

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Merge strategy
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `*.sln merge=union` is deliberately NOT set, even though it's a common
# template default. `union` keeps lines from both sides of a conflict with
# no conflict markers at all. For a structured, GUID-indexed format like
# .sln (paired Project/EndProject blocks, nested GlobalSection entries under
# NestedProjects), a silent union merge can produce a file that still LOADS
# in Visual Studio but is semantically wrong - duplicated project entries,
# or a NestedProjects section referencing a GUID that only exists on one
# side. That kind of corruption is worse than a normal merge conflict,
# because nothing flags it for review. A real conflict that stops and forces
# a human to look at the .sln is the safer default. If solution-file
# conflicts turn out to be frequent (e.g. many contributors adding projects
# in parallel), revisit this - but don't default to silent merging.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Binary files
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3D print files (stl/)
*.stl       binary

# Datasheets / product briefs (docs/)
*.pdf       binary

# Images
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary

# Fonts
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary

# Native binaries / libraries
*.dll       binary
*.exe       binary
*.so        binary
*.dylib     binary

# Signing / certificate material - content must never be touched or diffed
*.snk       binary
*.pfx       binary

# ML model formats used by this project
*.litertlm  binary
*.tflite    binary
*.gguf      binary
*.onnx      binary
*.bin       binary

# Audio
*.wav       binary
*.mp3       binary
*.ogg       binary
*.flac      binary

# SVG - deliberately NOT treated as binary.
# SVG is XML text, not a binary image format. Marking it `binary` would
# block meaningful diffs and blame on hand-authored/icon-tweak changes and
# would defeat git's normal text delta compression for no reason. Treated as
# text and LF-normalized like other source. Revisit per-file/per-directory
# only if this project ever vendors minified/data-URI-embedded SVGs where a
# textual diff is useless anyway.
*.svg       text eol=lf

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Linguist overrides (GitHub language statistics)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Note: *.stl and *.pdf are already `binary` above, and GitHub Linguist
# already excludes binary files from language percentages by itself - so,
# as of today, neither folder strictly needs an override to keep language
# stats clean. Overrides below are added anyway to be explicit and to
# future-proof against non-binary files (e.g. a markdown spec sheet) later
# landing in these folders and unexpectedly skewing the language graph.
#
# stl/ holds original 3D-printable enclosure parts. It is neither
# documentation nor vendored/third-party code, so neither
# linguist-documentation nor linguist-vendored is semantically honest for
# it - forcing one on would mislabel what these files are. No linguist
# override is applied for stl/*; the `binary` attribute above already keeps
# it out of language stats, which is the actual goal.
#
# docs/ holds reference material (component datasheets, product briefs).
# That does fit linguist-documentation's intent, so it's marked explicitly.
docs/*      linguist-documentation

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Existing tracked source (must keep working as-is)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
frontend/src/**/*.jsx   text eol=lf diff=javascript
frontend/src/**/*.js    text eol=lf diff=javascript
backend/*.py            text eol=lf diff=python
